  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 338 AM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nLast of the showers/storms in far SE areas have finally moved\naway. Focus shifts to a cool front located from NE Wisconsin to\neastern IA. A few showers and storms have been observed in \neastern IA as well as over Lk MI, but are struggling at this \npoint. For the most part, the front should pass through with \nlittle overall fanfare, but a few showers/storms could pop up \nalong the front as it drops SE with the best chances closer to \nI-69 and points east. Have kept pops confined to slgt chc to \nchc. Behind the front, slightly cooler and less humid air will \narrive for Sat night into early Sunday. \n\nSigns continue to point towards a potentially active pattern\nstarting as early as Sunday afternoon and persisting into\nTuesday with both heavy rain and severe weather in play as the\ncool front stalls and then pushes back north as a warm front in\nresponse to low pressure moving into WI by 00Z Mon. An unstable\nenvironment will be in place Sunday afternoon and evening,\nalthough models differ on the degree of instability and shear.\nIn addition, evolution of convective development varies with\nsome developing in the afternoon or evening and others develop\nupstream across WI and move in during the evening or overnight\nhours. DCAPE should be in the 1000 to 1200 J/KG with at least 25\nto 30 kts of shear. SPC has upgraded to a slight risk (2 out of\n5) from eastern IA into the western half of the forecast area,\nmainly for a wind threat and non zero chance for an isolated\ntornado. Given the concerns for the exact evolution of\nconvection, have not made many changes with focus on late\nafternoon into evening period. Potential for training of\nconvection in a high PWAT environment could lead to some\nflooding concerns with WPC maintaining the Slight Risk of\nExcessive rain across the area. \n\nThe frontal boundary lingers across the same general area into\nTuesday and possibly Wednesday, with its position each day\nlikely influenced by impacts of subtle waves, MCVs and outflow\nboundaries making for a challenging forecast. Slight Risk of\nsevere (focus on damaging winds) and Excessive Rainfall \ncontinues across the forecast area. Way too many variables that\nfar out to mess with anything in the forecast.",
